# Copy to .env and edit. Used by docker-compose/docker-compose.yml (the PostgREST stack).

# =============================================================================
# The stack ships its OWN identity provider — the `idp` service (semantius-idp),
# mounted at /idp on the front door and sharing this stack's Postgres. It is the
# DEFAULT issuer, so there is nothing to point at an external IdP for a working
# first run: the two VITE_OAUTH_* vars below default to it, PostgREST validates
# tokens against its JWKS, and http://localhost:3000/idp serves a setup page on
# first boot where you create the first administrator.
#
# The one thing you MUST set is IDP_SECRET (see "Bundled identity provider"
# below) — it ships with a dev default here, like POSTGRES_PASSWORD does.
#
# Going live: set IDP_BASE_URL + PUBLIC_WEB_ORIGIN to your domain (and the
# passwords). To replace the bundled issuer with your own, set all three of
# VITE_OAUTH_CONFIG, VITE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID and JWKS_URL — see below.
# =============================================================================
# VITE_OAUTH_CONFIG    — the OIDC discovery URL. The admin SPA reads its OAuth
#                        endpoints from it, AND jwks-fetch derives the JWKS from
#                        it when JWKS_URL is empty. Defaults to the bundled idp's
#                        document, served from the front door's ORIGIN ROOT.
# VITE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID — the public OAuth client id. Defaults to `public-client`,
#                        the SPA registered in idp-config/oauth_clients.jsonc.
# Both are commented out: the compose defaults already point at the bundled idp
# (and follow WEB_PORT). Uncomment and edit to use a DIFFERENT issuer — then set
# JWKS_URL empty as well, so the keys are derived from that issuer's discovery.
# VITE_OAUTH_CONFIG=https://your-issuer.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration
# VITE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=public-client

# VITE_OAUTH_AUDIENCE — the RFC 8707 resource the SPA asks its access tokens to
# be issued FOR; it becomes their `aud`. The SPA ALWAYS sends one, falling back
# to a built-in placeholder when this is empty, and an issuer that validates
# resources rejects an unknown one — the symptom is a bare "Login Error" on
# /oauth2_callback. Defaults to IDP_BASE_URL, which is exactly what the bundled
# idp registers, so leave it alone unless you are replacing the issuer; then set
# the audience YOUR issuer expects (its issuer URL, if it has no separate one).
# VITE_OAUTH_AUDIENCE=https://your-issuer.example.com

# JWKS_URL — the keys PostgREST validates bearer tokens against. Defaults to the
# bundled idp reached IN-NETWORK: http://idp:3000/idp/.well-known/jwks.json —
# explicit rather than derived, because the `jwks_uri` the idp advertises carries
# its PUBLIC base URL, which the jwks-fetch container cannot resolve. Set it to
# EMPTY to auto-derive from VITE_OAUTH_CONFIG's discovery document instead, which
# is what an external issuer wants — UNCOMMENT the line below and leave it blank;
# the compose only falls back to the bundled idp when the variable is absent
# entirely. PostgREST can't fetch a JWKS URL itself, so jwks-fetch downloads it
# ONCE at startup into a file; after a key rotation run ./jwks-refresh.sh.
# JWKS_URL=

# --- Bundled identity provider (the `idp` service) ---------------------------

# IDP_SECRET — signs sessions AND encrypts the JWT signing keys at rest. At least
# 32 random bytes; generate one with:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# The value below is a DEV DEFAULT so the stack starts out of the box (the same
# philosophy as POSTGRES_PASSWORD) — replace it for anything shared or exposed.
# ROTATING IT IS NOT FREE: it logs every user out AND makes the stored signing
# keys undecryptable, so plan it as a key rotation, not a config tweak.
IDP_SECRET=dev-only-idp-secret-change-me-0123456789abcdef

# Version tag of the identity provider image (ghcr.io/semantius/semantius-idp).
# Re-pulled on every create/up (pull_policy: always); pin to a released tag for a
# reproducible/server deploy.
SEMANTIUS_IDP_VERSION=latest

# IDP_PULL_POLICY / SEMANTIUS_IDP_VERSION — how the idp image is resolved.
# The default re-pulls the released tag on every create/up. To run an image you
# built yourself (`pnpm docker:smoke` in semantius-idp tags `semantius-idp:local`;
# retag it under the ghcr name), point SEMANTIUS_IDP_VERSION at that tag AND set
# IDP_PULL_POLICY=missing — otherwise the pull replaces your build with the
# registry's copy of the same tag, or fails outright on a tag never pushed.
#   SEMANTIUS_IDP_VERSION=d97-local
#   IDP_PULL_POLICY=missing

# IDP_BASE_URL — the ISSUER: scheme + host[:port] + the /idp mount path, no
# trailing slash. Everything absolute the idp emits derives from it, and it is
# also the default `aud` of its tokens. Defaults to
# http://localhost:${WEB_PORT}/idp; set it to your public front door going live.
# (If you seed pg_semantius's `_settings.jwt_aud` to enforce the audience in-DB,
# seed it with EXACTLY this value.)
# IDP_BASE_URL=https://yourdomain.com/idp

# PUBLIC_WEB_ORIGIN — the ORIGIN of the admin SPA (no path, no trailing slash).
# idp-config/oauth_clients.jsonc builds the SPA's redirect URIs from it, and they
# are matched EXACTLY, so it must be the origin the browser really uses. Defaults
# to http://localhost:${WEB_PORT}.
# PUBLIC_WEB_ORIGIN=https://yourdomain.com

# RESEND_API_KEY — optional. Without it the idp runs in DEGRADED mode: password
# reset, e-mail verification and every notification are disabled and hidden in
# the UI. Fine for local dev, where an admin creates the accounts from
# /idp/admin; set it (and a real `from` in idp-config/config.jsonc) for anything
# people depend on.
# RESEND_API_KEY=

# --- Everything below has a working default ----------------------------------

# Password for the `postgres` full-DBA login (used by DATABASE_URL / the CLI, and
# by the idp for both its connections). Dev default; change it for anything
# shared or network-exposed. Keep it URL-SAFE — no @ : / ? # or spaces: it is
# spliced into connection URLs for the idp and for pgbouncer, whose entrypoint
# parses them with a naive grep/cut parser.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=postgres

# Database the server creates and migrations deploy into. The idp shares it, in
# its own `idp` schema.
POSTGRES_DB=semantius

# Password for `semantius_authenticator` — the role PostgREST logs in as. It is
# NOSUPERUSER/NOINHERIT and can only SET ROLE authenticated / anon. Read by the
# image's baked 20-authenticator-login.sh AND passed to PostgREST (PGRST_DB_URI) —
# keep the two in sync. Dev default; change it for anything shared or exposed.
# URL-SAFE as well (same reason as POSTGRES_PASSWORD above).
SEMANTIUS_AUTHENTICATOR_PASSWORD=devpassword

# Version tag of the Semantius DB image (ghcr.io/semantius/postgres) to run.
# create/up PULL this tag from GHCR by default; `create.sh --build` builds from
# ./extension and tags :latest locally instead. Pin to a released tag (e.g.
# 0.3.0-pg18) for reproducible/server deploys — released tags always carry the
# -pg<major> suffix; only latest/latest-pg<major> move.
SEMANTIUS_DB_VERSION=latest

# Version tag of the admin SPA image (ghcr.io/semantius/semantius-app) to run.
# It is re-pulled from GHCR on every create (pull_policy: always); pin to a
# released tag for a reproducible/server deploy.
SEMANTIUS_APP_VERSION=latest

# Optional Northwind demo module. Set to ANY non-empty value (e.g. TRUE) to load
# it on first init; leave unset/empty for an empty database. Only takes effect on
# a FRESH data volume (the image's init scripts run once, at DB creation).
NWIND=TRUE

# Host ports. 5432=pgdocker CLI, 5433=pgdocker ext, 5434=this stack, 6432=pgbouncer
# (all side by side).
#
# WEB_PORT is the CADDY FRONT DOOR — the single entry point for the browser:
# `/` the admin SPA, `/rest/*` the PostgREST API, `/api-docs/*` the Scalar docs
# (both prefixes stripped) and `/idp/*` + `/.well-known/*` the identity provider
# (prefix KEPT — it is part of the issuer URL). POSTGREST_PORT, DOCS_PORT and
# IDP_PORT publish those services directly as well, which is convenient in local
# dev; the SPA has no host port of its own — it is only reachable through caddy.
# WEB_PORT is 3000 — the port a browser is conventionally pointed at, and the
# front door is the only thing here a human opens. IDP_PORT sits at 3001 and
# POSTGREST_PORT well clear at 3100 (its CONTAINER port stays 3000): a Vite dev
# server told to use 3000 walks 3001, 3002, ... until something is free, so a
# host port parked in that range collides with a dev server sooner or later —
# semantius-idp/apps/web is exactly such a server. Both are for poking at the
# containers only: signing in has to go through the front door, because that is
# the origin the idp's own URLs carry.
POSTGRES_PORT=5434
PGBOUNCER_PORT=6432
POSTGREST_PORT=3100
IDP_PORT=3001
DOCS_PORT=8080
WEB_PORT=3000

# SITE_ADDRESS — the address Caddy serves inside the container. The default,
# ":80", is plain HTTP: right for local dev and for anything that terminates TLS
# in front of the stack (Dokploy/Traefik). On a BARE VPS put your bare domain here
# and Caddy obtains a certificate automatically — then publish 80:80 and 443:443
# on the caddy service in docker-compose.yml instead of WEB_PORT.
# SITE_ADDRESS=yourdomain.com

# Browser-reachable base URL of the PostgREST API AS THE DOCS SEE IT. Used for BOTH
# the OpenAPI spec's advertised server (PGRST_OPENAPI_SERVER_PROXY_URI) and the
# Scalar docs' spec `url` — both resolved by the browser, so NEVER the in-network
# `postgrest` hostname. Leave unset for local dev: it defaults to this stack's OWN
# front door, http://localhost:${WEB_PORT}/gateway/rest. Going live, set the PUBLIC
# front-door URL INCLUDING that prefix, e.g. https://yourdomain.com/gateway/rest.
#
# /gateway/rest and NOT /rest: both reach the same PostgREST, but /gateway/rest goes
# through the idp's authenticating proxy, which exchanges an `x-api-key` for a
# bearer token — so the docs' "Test Request" works with an API key from
# /idp/account/api-keys instead of a hand-minted JWT. The admin SPA uses the direct
# /rest route (VITE_API_BASE_URL in docker-compose.yml); it already holds an OAuth
# access token and gains nothing from the extra hop.
# PUBLIC_API_URL=https://yourdomain.com/gateway/rest

# After `docker compose up`, the full-DBA connection string is:
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}@localhost:${POSTGRES_PORT}/${POSTGRES_DB}
#
# The POOLED endpoint (PgBouncer, transaction pooling) for external apps that use
# the SET LOCAL ROLE pattern — pools `semantius_authenticator`, not the DBA login:
#   postgresql://semantius_authenticator:${SEMANTIUS_AUTHENTICATOR_PASSWORD}@localhost:${PGBOUNCER_PORT}/${POSTGRES_DB}
